{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792718071,"nonce":"ICaUv8zvrB7eDFDozuXBQ7dTENU=","title":"SSA: an element assignment drops its value's control flow when the value is a conditional","message":"An assignment `xs[i] = a if c else b` followed by more statements in the same function loses the conditional's blocks: the statements after it are emitted into the block before the branch, and the merge block with the store is orphaned. The function then stores the wrong value or never stores. Introduced by 63f980f9, which evaluates the array and index operands of an element store with `translate_operand`.\n\nCause: `translate_assignment` evaluates the right-hand side, then `settle` leaves a pending `continuation_block` for its caller. The element-store arm's own `translate_operand` calls then take that pending report as if their operand had made it, so the caller never learns where the statement ended. `translate_operand` now reads only the report its operand's evaluation makes and restores any report that was already pending.\n\nSeen as: Python conformance set/dynamic_operand.py (comparisons of a typed set against a dynamic one returned False) and list/assign_conditional_value.py (new, a self-contained case). Bisected over aaa974bf to 266926d9 on the `set` tests; 63f980f9 is the first bad commit. One of three independent causes of the Python failures in 837213a; the others are 0d9f2bc0 (interpreter, 837213a) and the signature typing behind 8ff7ff4.","files":null}]}